  <div class="wrap">
<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Ensuring assets are not carried above their recoverable amount</div><div class="principle">ASBE No. 8 governs the IMPAIRMENT of FIXED assets, CONSTRUCTION in PROGRESS, INTANGIBLE assets, GOODWILL, LONG-term equity INVESTMENTS (under the EQUITY method), and BIOLOGICAL assets CARRIED at cost â€” ENSURING these ASSETS are NOT carried ABOVE their RECOVERABLE amount. This Standard CONTAINS the SINGLE most FREQUENTLY cited ASBE-specific DIVERGENCE from international STANDARDS: the ABSOLUTE prohibition on REVERSING impairment LOSSES once RECOGNISED (Section 8).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Long-lived assets, excluding inventories and financial assets</div><div class="principle">This Standard applies to the ASSET categories LISTED above â€” EXCLUDING inventories (GOVERNED separately by ASBE No. 1, WHICH permits REVERSAL â€” see the COMPANION poster), investment PROPERTY measured UNDER the fair VALUE model, deferred TAX assets, and FINANCIAL assets (governed SEPARATELY by ASBE No. 22).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Indicators of Impairment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">External and internal signs that an asset may be impaired</div><div class="principle">At EACH Balance SHEET date, an ENTERPRISE assesses WHETHER any INDICATION exists that an ASSET may be IMPAIRED â€” EXTERNAL indicators (SIGNIFICANT declines in MARKET value, ADVERSE changes in the TECHNOLOGICAL, market, ECONOMIC, or LEGAL environment) and INTERNAL indicators (EVIDENCE of OBSOLESCENCE or PHYSICAL damage, SIGNIFICANT changes in HOW an asset is USED). WHERE such an INDICATOR exists, the ASSET'S recoverable AMOUNT is ESTIMATED.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Recoverable Amount â€” the Higher of Two Values</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Fair value less costs to sell, or value in use</div><div class="principle">RECOVERABLE amount is the HIGHER of an ASSET'S FAIR value LESS costs to SELL, and its VALUE IN USE (the PRESENT value of the FUTURE cash FLOWS expected to be DERIVED from the asset's CONTINUING use and EVENTUAL disposal) â€” CONSISTENT with IAS 36's OWN definition internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Recognising an Impairment Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Carrying amount less recoverable amount</div><div class="principle">WHERE an ASSET'S carrying AMOUNT EXCEEDS its RECOVERABLE amount, an IMPAIRMENT loss is RECOGNISED for the DIFFERENCE, REDUCING the asset's CARRYING amount to its RECOVERABLE amount, WITH the loss RECOGNISED immediately in the INCOME STATEMENT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Cash-Generating Units</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Testing groups of assets where individual cash flows can't be isolated</div><div class="principle">WHERE an ASSET does NOT generate CASH inflows LARGELY independently of OTHER assets, IMPAIRMENT is assessed at the LEVEL of the SMALLEST identifiable GROUP of assets that DOES generate LARGELY independent cash INFLOWS â€” a "CASH-GENERATING UNIT" (CGU) â€” CONSISTENT with IAS 36's OWN CGU concept internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Goodwill â€” Mandatory Annual Testing, Allocated to CGUs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Tested annually regardless of impairment indicators</div><div class="principle">GOODWILL arising from a BUSINESS combination (ASBE No. 20) is ALLOCATED to the CASH-generating UNITS (or GROUPS of units) EXPECTED to BENEFIT from the COMBINATION'S synergies, and TESTED for IMPAIRMENT AT LEAST ANNUALLY, REGARDLESS of WHETHER any INDICATOR of impairment EXISTS â€” CONSISTENT with the IMPAIRMENT-only approach (NO systematic AMORTISATION) applied under IFRS 3/IAS 36 internationally, a POINT of GENUINE ALIGNMENT with IFRS, in CONTRAST to Japan GAAP's OWN mandatory GOODWILL amortisation requirement.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE â€” No Reversal of Impairment Losses</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Once recognised, an impairment loss is permanent</div><div class="principle">ONCE an IMPAIRMENT loss is RECOGNISED for an ASSET within THIS Standard's scope, it CANNOT be REVERSED in a SUBSEQUENT period â€” EVEN if the RECOVERABLE amount SUBSEQUENTLY INCREASES (e.g. due to a MARKET recovery or IMPROVED operating PERFORMANCE). This is a GENUINELY significant, WIDELY-cited DIVERGENCE from IAS 36, which PERMITS reversal of IMPAIRMENT losses for ASSETS OTHER than goodwill WHERE the CIRCUMSTANCES that CAUSED the ORIGINAL impairment have CHANGED.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Why ASBE Prohibits Reversal</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Preventing earnings management through impairment timing</div><div class="principle">The POLICY rationale WIDELY cited for this PROHIBITION reflects a CONCERN that PERMITTING reversal COULD create an OPPORTUNITY for EARNINGS management â€” an ENTITY might be TEMPTED to RECOGNISE an impairment LOSS in a WEAK period, then REVERSE it LATER to BOOST reported PROFIT in a STRONGER period, WITHOUT any GENUINE underlying ECONOMIC change having OCCURRED. PROHIBITING reversal REMOVES this INCENTIVE, at the COST of SOME reduced RESPONSIVENESS to GENUINE asset value RECOVERIES.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example â€” Recognising an Impairment Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Writing an asset down to its recoverable amount</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A production facility affected by a market downturn</div><div class="wk-body">A company's production facility has a carrying amount of RMB 40,000,000. Following a significant market downturn, the estimated recoverable amount (the higher of value in use and fair value less costs to sell) is RMB 28,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company recognises an IMPAIRMENT loss of RMB 40,000,000 âˆ’ RMB 28,000,000 = <strong>RMB 12,000,000</strong>, reducing the facility's carrying amount to RMB 28,000,000, with the loss recognised immediately in the INCOME STATEMENT.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example â€” a Later Recovery That Cannot Be Reversed</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The recovered value simply isn't recognised</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A market recovery in a later period</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, two years later the market recovers strongly, and the facility's recoverable amount is now estimated at RMB 36,000,000 â€” well above its current carrying amount (after depreciation) of RMB 24,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the CLEAR recovery in RECOVERABLE amount, the company <strong>CANNOT reverse</strong> ANY portion of the PRIOR impairment LOSS â€” the facility CONTINUES to be CARRIED at its (depreciated) POST-impairment amount, WITH the RMB 12,000,000-plus of RECOVERED value simply going UNRECOGNISED, a DIRECT consequence of this Standard's NO-REVERSAL rule.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBE No. 8 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Impairment losses recognised during the period, by class of asset and cash-generating unit</span><span>Basis for estimating recoverable amount (fair value less costs to sell, or value in use)</span><span>Goodwill impairment testing results and key assumptions used</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying the appropriate cash-generating unit for testing purposes</span><span>Whether an impairment indicator genuinely exists</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Future cash flow projections and discount rate underlying value in use</span><span>Fair value less costs to sell estimates</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 36 and US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison â€” ASBE 8 vs IAS 36 vs US GAAP (ASC 360)</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASBE 8</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 36</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-success);">US GAAP</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Impairment reversal (non-goodwill)</span><span>NOT permitted</span><span>PERMITTED where circumstances change</span><span>NOT permitted for held-and-use assets â€” aligned with ASBE</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Goodwill treatment</span><span>Impairment-only, no amortisation</span><span>Impairment-only â€” converged</span><span>Impairment-only â€” converged</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">ON the NO-REVERSAL rule, ASBE is GENUINELY aligned with US GAAP AGAINST IAS 36 â€” a USEFUL, THREE-way comparison POINT showing this is NOT simply "China DIVERGING from the INTERNATIONAL standard," but RATHER a CASE where TWO major FRAMEWORKS (US GAAP and ASBE) SHARE the SAME conservative APPROACH, WHILE IFRS takes a MORE flexible POSITION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every impairment event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Impairment loss</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised immediately, permanent â€” no future reversal</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 1</span><span>Inventories â€” governs the DIFFERENT, reversal-permitted write-down treatment for inventories, outside this Standard's scope</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 20</span><span>Business Combinations â€” supplies the goodwill this Standard requires to be tested annually</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Recoverable amount</div><div class="glossary-def">The higher of an asset's fair value less costs to sell and its value in use.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Cash-generating unit (CGU)</div><div class="glossary-def">The smallest identifiable group of assets that generates cash inflows largely independent of other assets, used as the level for impairment testing where individual assets can't be assessed alone.</div></div>
</div>
</div>
